// A small house, as a worked example of specifying automation in candyspeak.
//
// Read it as three sections: what the house HAS, what should HAPPEN, and what
// must NEVER happen. The third is the one other automation systems do not ask
// for, and it is what a model checker can later be pointed at.
//
// Timer values are shortened so the panel is watchable -- they are #param, so
// `> HallHold = 120` at the prompt sets two minutes without a rebuild.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the house

// sensors
#digital HallMotion   in pulldown 2      // 1 = someone in the hall
#digital BathMotion   in pulldown 3
#digital FrontDoor    in pulldown 4      // 1 = open
#digital LivingWindow in pulldown 5      // 1 = open
#digital NightButton  in pulldown 6      // by the bed: goodnight
#digital ArmButton    in pulldown 7      // arm / disarm the alarm

#analog  Daylight:10  in 0:0             // outdoor light sensor
#analog  IndoorTemp:10 in 0:1            // 0..1023, tenths of a degree

// lights
#digital HallLight    out 8
#digital KitchenLight out 9
#digital LivingLight  out 10
#digital BedLight     out 11
#digital BathLight    out 12
#digital OutdoorLamp  out 13

// the rest of the house
#digital BathFan__Blue   out 14
#digital Heater__Orange  out 15
#digital Siren__Red      out 16
#digital CoffeeMaker  out 17

// ---- how the panel should show it -------------------------------------
//
// Presentation is NOT in the language: nothing in `#digital NightButton in 6'
// says whether that is a momentary button or a latching switch, and it should
// not. #annotate says it next to the declaration, the compiler skips the line
// entirely, and the panel is the tool that owns these keys.

#annotate panel NightButton  kind=push
#annotate panel ArmButton    kind=push
#annotate panel HallMotion   kind=push     // motion is a pulse, not a switch
#annotate panel BathMotion   kind=push

#annotate panel Siren__Red   kind=action   // it sounds; it does not indicate
#annotate panel BathFan__Blue kind=action
#annotate panel Heater__Orange kind=action

#annotate panel IndoorTemp   kind=dial unit="0.1C"
#annotate panel Daylight     kind=slider

// what an operator is allowed to tune, live, without a rebuild
#param HallHold   = 3           // seconds the hall stays lit
#param FanRun     = 5           // seconds the fan runs on after the bathroom
#param SetPoint   = 210         // wanted indoor temperature, tenths
#param Hysteresis = 10          // +/- band, so the heater does not chatter
#param DarkBelow  = 300         // daylight reading that counts as dark

#timer HallTimer  3000
#timer FanTimer   5000

#variable Armed:1 = 0
#variable WinWas:1 = 0           // last cycle's window and alarm, for the net
#variable ArmWas:1  = 0
// FAILSAFE is NOT declared here: INIT=0, NORMAL=1 and FAILSAFE=2 are built in,
// installed by csp_rt_init, and user states are numbered from 3 up. Naming it
// again would be redundant -- `State = FAILSAFE` already resolves to the
// built-in one.
#states Home Night Away

// ------------------------------------------------------------ what happens

#in INIT
    State = Home
    HallTimer.period = HallHold * 1000
    FanTimer.period  = FanRun * 1000
#end

// The hall lights on movement and stays lit for a while after it stops.
//
// TWO things about this shape, both of which were got wrong first:
//
//   `= 1 ?` is deliberate. A guarded rule does nothing when its guard is
//   false, so the light LATCHES on and something else has to put it out.
//
//   The timer starts when the motion STOPS, not when it starts. `T = 1` only
//   arms a STOPPED timer, so arming it on every cycle of a held signal does
//   not extend it -- it expires while you are still standing there, and
//   timeout() is edge-triggered so the chance does not come back. Counting
//   down from the moment the room goes quiet is both what works and what a
//   person means by "stays on for a few seconds after".
#in Home Night Away
    HallLight = 1 ? HallMotion
    HallTimer = 1 ? !HallMotion && HallLight
    HallLight = 0 ? timeout(HallTimer)

    // The bathroom fan runs on after you leave -- the point of the timer.
    BathLight = 1 ? BathMotion
    BathFan__Blue = 1 ? BathMotion
    FanTimer  = 1 ? !BathMotion && BathFan__Blue
    BathLight = 0 ? timeout(FanTimer)
    BathFan__Blue = 0 ? timeout(FanTimer)

    // Outdoor lamp on darkness, with a band so dusk does not flicker it.
    OutdoorLamp = 1 ? Daylight < DarkBelow
    OutdoorLamp = 0 ? Daylight > DarkBelow + 50

    // Heating, likewise banded.
    Heater__Orange = 1 ? IndoorTemp < SetPoint - Hysteresis
    Heater__Orange = 0 ? IndoorTemp > SetPoint + Hysteresis

    // The alarm is a toggle on one button.
    Armed = 1 ? ArmButton && !Armed
    Armed = 0 ? ArmButton && Armed
#end

// Goodnight: everything social goes off, the bedroom keeps a reading light.
//
// INSIDE a block, because a loose rule -- one with no #in at all -- runs only in
// INIT and NORMAL. Written outside these braces this transition never fired:
// the house was in Home, so the rule was never even evaluated. Same trap as the
// window guard, one file apart.
#in Home
    State = Night ? NightButton
#end

#in Night
    KitchenLight = 0
    LivingLight  = 0
    CoffeeMaker  = 0
#end

#in Home
    // Morning coffee is a convenience, not a rule that fights you: it only
    // comes on when the house is Home and someone is actually up.
    CoffeeMaker = 1 ? HallMotion && Daylight > DarkBelow
#end

// ------------------------------------------------- what must never happen
//
// These are the invariants. candyspeak has no `#invariant` yet, so for now they
// are written as rules that FORCE the safe value.
//
// TWO things make that fragile, and both bit this file while it was written:
//
//  1. They must come LAST, because a later rule in the same cycle wins.
//  2. They must be INSIDE an #in block naming every state. A loose rule -- one
//     with no #in at all -- runs only in INIT and NORMAL, so written outside
//     these braces the window check never ran in Home and the heater happily
//     warmed the street.
//
// Which is the argument for `#invariant` as its own declaration: a property
// should not depend on where in the file it sits or which states someone
// remembered to list.

#in Home Night Away
    // No heating into an open window.
    Heater__Orange = 0 ? LivingWindow

    // The siren sounds only while armed, and only a door does it.
    Siren__Red = 1 ? Armed && FrontDoor
    Siren__Red = 0 ? !Armed
#end

// ------------------------------------------------------- the safety net
//
// The rules above PREVENT the two unsafe combinations. These DETECT them, and
// go to FAILSAFE rather than patching the output.
//
// That is the shape worth having, because it turns three scattered guards into
// ONE property: "FAILSAFE is unreachable". That is a sentence a model checker
// takes -- varp's `invariant State != FAILSAFE` -- and proving it proves the
// prevention above is complete. Patched outputs prove nothing: they hide the
// violation instead of ruling it out.
// ONE CYCLE OF GRACE, and it is not optional.
//
// Rules read the COMMITTED state. In the cycle the window opens, prevention
// sets Heater to 0 -- but this rule still sees the 1 that was committed at the
// end of the previous cycle, so a plain `Heater && LivingWindow` fires FAILSAFE
// every single time the window is opened in a warm house. It did, first try.
//
// So the net only closes on a violation that PERSISTS: the condition has to
// hold now AND to have held last cycle, by which time prevention has had its
// turn. WinWas/ArmWas are written last in the block, so reading them gives the
// previous cycle's value.
#in Home Night Away
    State = FAILSAFE ? Heater__Orange && LivingWindow && WinWas
    State = FAILSAFE ? Siren__Red && !Armed && !ArmWas

    WinWas = LivingWindow
    ArmWas = Armed
#end

// And if we do land here, land softly: everything that can hurt goes off,
// everything that helps you find your way goes on. A failsafe that simply
// stops is not safe in a dark house.
#in FAILSAFE
    Heater__Orange = 0
    Siren__Red     = 0
    CoffeeMaker    = 0
    HallLight      = 1
    OutdoorLamp    = 1
#end
